## Who can use it

The 23 November post does not name countries, languages or a subscription for the Wallet connection. Google's help page on AI Mode personalization adds that connecting Google content apps is for users aged 18 or older. It also says personalization needs history and personalized recommendations switched on. The computer help page also requires a personal Google Account.

That computer page publishes a list of countries, territories and languages where connecting Google content apps to Search is available, so check it for yours. In the version we read on 26 November, the United States, Canada, India, Pakistan, Australia and Qatar were listed, while the United Kingdom, Germany, France, Italy and Spain were not. The page does not mention Wallet, so it does not settle where the Wallet connection works.

The help pages do not yet line up neatly. The AI Mode page lists Gmail, Calendar, Drive, Google Photos and Wallet, with the Gemini app marked as coming soon, even though the announcement says Drive is still to come. A separate computer help page still lists Gmail and Calendar as the available Workspace apps, calls other Workspace apps coming soon, and does not list Wallet. It also says Calendar is limited to English in the United States, and that Photos is not available in every region. Google notes that its Search settings are being updated, so your screen may differ, and that app connections are reaching users gradually. If you do not see an option, check the age, account type, country and history requirements.

## How to turn it on or off

The announcement gives this route:

1. Open Search Personalization in your Google Account.
2. Choose Connected Apps.
3. Pick the apps you want to connect.

To disconnect, return to Connected Apps and switch the app off. Google says connecting is your choice and can be reversed at any time. The computer help page also says Personalized Recommendations must be on, and describes extra settings for Gmail (smart features) and Photos (Face Groups and estimated locations).

Disconnecting is not the same as deleting. The computer help page says that disconnecting an app, or deleting data in it, does not delete anything from your Search Services History, and that this data can still be used to improve Google's products, including to train generative AI models. You can manage and delete that history separately at activity.google.com. You can still use AI Mode without connecting any content apps.

## What it can read, and what Google says about training

For Wallet, the announcement states that AI Mode can only read information from your accounts. In Google's words, it "can't move, manage, or invest your money."

Read-only access still exposes sensitive detail, and the data statements matter. According to the computer help page, your Gmail inbox, Drive files, Calendar, other Workspace content and Photos imagery or audio are not fed directly into generative AI training in Search. Material derived from them is treated differently. Summaries, excerpts, generated media and inferences can be used to answer prompts, and Google trains models on them, as well as on your Search questions and the responses. A summary of a short or especially relevant email or file may contain all of it.

Google says it takes steps to reduce personal information from personalized interactions before training, unless it is addressing abuse or harm. Human reviewers, including trained service providers, see a subset of this data. Before service providers review it, Google says it takes steps such as disconnecting it from your account, with the same exception.

The same page advises against connecting content apps that hold personal or confidential information you would not want used to train generative AI models or seen by a reviewer.

Two cautions follow. That page's training wording does not mention Wallet, and we found no Wallet-specific training statement for Search in the pages we read. Also, the AI Mode help page says consenting lets all Search services, such as Maps, Shopping and Flights, access Workspace and Photos. Think about that wider reach before connecting a Drive full of client files. This is general information, not financial or legal advice.

## Two things with similar names

**Wallet in the Gemini app** is a separate launch, announced on 13 November for Gemini app users in the United States. Gemini Apps Help says you must be 18 or over, signed in with a personal account rather than a work or school one, with history on, and that it works in English only. It can use bank and card accounts linked through Plaid, a service that connects financial accounts. Those rules are published for Gemini, so do not assume they apply to AI Mode.

**Connected Apps for actions** in Search is also different. The Android help page describes linking services such as Instacart, Canva and YouTube Music so AI Mode can help fill a cart, start a design or build a playlist. It says this is available in the United States in English.

## Before you connect: a short checklist

- Decide which app adds real value. You can choose them one at a time.
- Check what sits in your Drive, including shared files about other people.
- Keep work material out unless your employer or clients allow it.
- Treat money suggestions as a starting point and verify the figures in your bank or card statements.
- Revisit the Connected Apps setting after a few weeks and remove anything you are not using.

The questions to watch are when Drive actually appears, which countries get the Wallet connection and whether Google publishes data terms written for Wallet in Search.
